Airbnb Price Prediction - NYC

 Problem Statement
Airbnb hosts in NYC struggle to price their listings competitively. This project builds a machine learning model that predicts optimal nightly prices based on listing features.
 Dataset
Source: NYC Airbnb Open Data (Kaggle)
Size: ~48,000 listings
Features: Location, room type, reviews, availability, etc.
 Approach
1. Data Cleaning: Removed outliers (price > $1000), handled missing values
2. EDA: Analyzed price distribution, neighborhood trends, and feature correlations
3. Feature Engineering: Created popularity score, superhost flag, days since review
4. Models Tested: Linear Regression, Ridge, Lasso, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, XGBoost
5. Best Model: Gradient Boosting (R² = 0.68, RMSE = $82)

Results

| Model | RMSE | R² Score |
| Gradient Boosting | $82.50 | 0.68 |
| Random Forest | $84.20 | 0.66 |
| XGBoost | $85.10 | 0.65 |
| Linear Regression | $120.30 | 0.45 |

Key Insights
Neighborhood: Manhattan is the most expensive borough
Room Type: Entire apartments cost ~2x more than private rooms
Reviews: More reviews → slightly higher prices (trust factor)
Availability: Lower availability → higher prices (scarcity)
